/**
 * Styles for the {{ appName }} SPA.
 *
 * Tailwind v4 and daisyUI v5 are both configured from CSS — there is no
 * tailwind.config.js and no PostCSS pipeline to keep in sync. `@import` pulls
 * Tailwind in, `@plugin` registers daisyUI, and `@source` tells Tailwind which
 * files to scan for class names (Svelte components included).
 */

/*
 * `source(none)`, and the `@source` line below is the whole source list.
 *
 * In Tailwind v4 `@source` **adds to** automatic detection rather than replacing it, and
 * detection starts at the git root — so this scanned `src/`, `app/`, `www/` and every
 * previous build output still on disk.
 *
 * Two costs, and the second is the one that wasted days. The built stylesheet carried
 * rules for classes that appear only in server-rendered `src/Views/*.php`: 63 KB, about
 * 30%, that the SPA bundle can never use — and in a hybrid project the two halves
 * deliberately have separate stylesheets, which is what `theme:build` is for.
 *
 * And **two builds of identical sources produced different filenames.** The bundle is
 * content-hashed and committed, so that reads as a non-deterministic build and was
 * investigated as one twice. The cause is the scan reaching over a VirtioFS bind mount,
 * where the container sees part of the tree a beat late and which part varies per run.
 *
 * Naming the sources and then scanning everything anyway is not a combination anybody
 * asks for. A project that wants the repository scanned has not written an `@source`.
 */
@import "tailwindcss" source(none);
/* The palette, generated from this project's server-rendered theme so both
   halves of the application share one set of colours. CSS requires @import
   before other rules, so the file wins on specificity rather than order — see
   the note in theme.css. */
@import "./theme.css";
@plugin "daisyui";

@source "./**/*.{svelte,js}";
